    "confidence": "low",
    "plain": "Making St George's and St David's Day bank holidays could boost national pride and shared celebration, but there is no solid evidence this translates into measurable improvements in social trust or community cohesion at population scale. The effect on belonging is speculative.",
    "biggest_unknown": "Whether national-day bank holidays actually increase inter-group trust and civic participation, or mainly benefit those who already identify strongly with the national symbol.",
    "claims": [
      {
        "tier": "projected",
        "claim": "Elevating these days to public holidays could foster a greater sense of national pride and encourage cultural events and celebrations.",
        "evidence_id": "E16",
        "quote": "Making these days public holidays would elevate their status, potentially fostering a greater sense of national pride and encouraging cultural events and celebrations"
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "St George's Day and St David's Day are not currently bank holidays, unlike patron saint days in other UK nations, so designating them would bring England and Wales in line with Scotland and Northern Ireland.",
        "evidence_id": "E19",
        "quote": "Designating St George's Day and St David's Day would bring England and Wales in line with other UK nations in celebrating their patron saints"
      }
    ],
    "rationale": "The only evidence directly touching O15 is E16, which uses explicitly speculative language ('potentially fostering'). The policy does commit a concrete mechanism \u2014 a statutory bank holiday \u2014 but a mechanism being plausible is not the same as evidence it fires at scale. There is no cited evidence that national-day holidays measurably improve social trust surveys, reduce loneliness, increase civic participation, or reduce inter-group tension in comparable real-world cases. The policy frames its goal around English national identity specifically; while that could generate shared celebration, it could equally leave out residents who do not identify with that symbol \u2014 but there is no cited evidence on either side of that sub-question, so it cannot drive the verdict. Given the sole O15-relevant claim is speculative and no baseline cohesion data or comparable natural experiments are provided, the honest verdict is that any effect is likely minor at most, with low confidence. The direction is assessed as negligible rather than too-uncertain because there is no cited evidence of a material countervailing harm either \u2014 both sides lack grounding, and the mechanism's scale effect is simply undemonstrated.",
    "time_split": {
      "near_direction": "negligible",
      "long_direction": "negligible",
      "note": "No evidence suggests cohesion effects accumulate or decay meaningfully over time versus an immediate symbolic boost."
    },
